<%- include('partials/header', { links, title: 'TAGR — How It Works', styles: `
.tgr-exp { max-width: 750px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 1.5rem; color: var(--text-light); }
.tgr-exp h1 { color: var(--accent-gold); font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 0.3rem; }
.tgr-exp .sub { color: #8b949e; font-size: 0.85rem; margin-bottom: 2rem; }
.tgr-exp h2 { color: var(--accent-green); font-size: 1.2rem; margin: 2rem 0 0.8rem; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); padding-bottom: 0.4rem; }
.tgr-exp p, .tgr-exp li { font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.7; color: #c9d1d9; }
.tgr-exp strong { color: var(--accent-gold); }
.tgr-exp ul { padding-left: 1.5rem; margin: 0.5rem 0; }
.tgr-exp li { margin-bottom: 0.3rem; }
.tgr-exp .formula-box { background: #1a1a1a; border: 1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1rem 1.2rem; margin: 1rem 0; font-family: monospace; font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--accent-gold); }
.tgr-exp .weight-table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 1rem 0; }
.tgr-exp .weight-table th { background: #2d2d2d; color: var(--accent-gold); padding: 0.6rem; text-align: left; font-size: 0.8rem; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border-color); }
.tgr-exp .weight-table td { padding: 0.5rem 0.6rem; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.05); font-size: 0.85rem; }
.tgr-exp .weight-table tr:hover { background: rgba(226,183,20,0.06); }
.tgr-exp .example { background: rgba(46,204,113,0.06); border: 1px solid rgba(46,204,113,0.15); border-radius: 8px; padding: 0.8rem 1rem; margin: 1rem 0; font-size: 0.85rem; }
.tgr-exp .example strong { color: #2ecc71; }
.tgr-exp .back-link { display: inline-block; margin-top: 2rem; color: var(--accent-gold); text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold; font-size: 0.9rem; }
.tgr-exp .back-link:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
.tgr-exp .badge { display: inline-block; padding: 0.15rem 0.5rem; border-radius: 4px; font-size: 0.7rem; font-weight: bold; }
.tgr-exp .badge.asa { background: rgba(46,204,113,0.15); color: #2ecc71; }
.tgr-exp .badge.tfaa { background: rgba(52,152,219,0.15); color: #3498db; }
.tgr-exp .badge.indoor { background: rgba(155,89,182,0.15); color: #9b59b6; }
` }) %>

<%
  // The weights come from the ranking code (tagr/engine.js), so this page always shows what is used.
  const times = n => n.toFixed(2) + '×';
  const asaUnknown = rules.weights['TX ASA'];
  const asaKnown = asaUnknown * rules.knownDistanceFactor;
  const knownCut = Math.round((1 - rules.knownDistanceFactor) * 100);
  const stateBonus = Math.round((rules.stateRoundBonus - 1) * 100);
  const records = rules.nationalRecords;
  const outdoorWeight = rules.weights['TFAA Outdoor'];
  // The worked example: a barebow recurve SYWAT round against the average of the two records.
  const barebowBar = (records.BBR.field + records.BBR.hunter) / 2;
  const barebowNps = Math.round(470 / barebowBar * 1000 * outdoorWeight);
  const freestyleNps = Math.round(540 / records.FS.field * 1000 * outdoorWeight);
  const pct = n => '+' + Math.round((n - 1) * 100) + '%';
  const asaClasses = Object.keys(asaBars).sort((a, b) => asaBars[b] - asaBars[a] || a.localeCompare(b));
  const recency = rules.recencyWeights;
%>
<section>
  <div class="tgr-exp">
    <h1>🏹 How TAGR Works</h1>
    <div class="sub">Texas Archery Global Ranking — Cross-Organization Normalized Performance Rankings (<%= new Date().getFullYear() - 2 %>–<%= new Date().getFullYear() %>)</div>

    <h2>📊 The NPS Formula</h2>
    <p>Every score from every organization is converted to a <strong>Normalized Performance Score (NPS)</strong>:</p>
    <div class="formula-box">NPS = (Your Score ÷ The Bar) × 1000 × Org Weight × Bonuses</div>
    <p><strong>The bar for a TFAA round is the national record for your style</strong>, from the NFAA (see <a href="#national-records" style="color:var(--accent-gold);">National Records</a> below). A round that ties the national record is worth 1000 before weights and bonuses, whatever bow you shoot, so great barebow, traditional and longbow shooting counts as much as great compound shooting. Texas ASA has no national records like that, so <strong>the bar for a 3D round is the best score anyone shot in the same class in the seasons TAGR reads</strong> (see <a href="#asa-bars" style="color:var(--accent-gold);">Texas ASA Class Bars</a> below). Nobody shoots a perfect 360, so measuring against it kept every 3D round well below a TFAA round. The distance weights and <a href="#equipment-bonuses" style="color:var(--accent-gold);">equipment bonuses</a> below make up for a harder class.</p>
    <p>NPS is <strong>uncapped</strong> — equipment bonuses, yardage bonuses, and format multipliers stack naturally. Top archers in difficult equipment classes and long-distance formats will earn NPS well above 1000, which properly differentiates elite performers.</p>

    <h2>⚖️ Organization Difficulty Weights</h2>
    <p>Different formats have different difficulty levels. Harder formats earn more NPS per point scored. The order is TFAA Outdoor, then TFAA Indoor, then Texas ASA.</p>
    <table class="weight-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>Weight</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><span class="badge tfaa">TFAA Outdoor</span> Field Archery</td><td><strong><%= times(rules.weights['TFAA Outdoor']) %></strong></td><td>Hardest — varying terrain, elevation, distances out to 80 yards</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="badge indoor">TFAA Indoor</span> 300 Round</td><td><strong><%= times(rules.weights['TFAA Indoor']) %></strong></td><td>20 yards and flat, but scored against a near-perfect national record, with X count on top</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="badge asa">TX ASA</span> 3D Unknown Distance</td><td><strong><%= times(asaUnknown) %></strong></td><td>Scored against the best score of the class, not a national record</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="badge asa">TX ASA</span> 3D Known Distance</td><td><strong><%= times(asaKnown) %></strong></td><td>Rangefinders allowed — removes yardage estimation (<%= knownCut %>% less than unknown distance)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>🎯 ASA Known vs Unknown Distance</h2>
    <p>ASA classes are split into <strong>known distance</strong> (rangefinders allowed) and <strong>unknown distance</strong> (judge yardage by eye). Unknown distance classes get the full <%= times(asaUnknown) %> weight; known distance classes get <%= knownCut %>% less (<%= times(asaKnown) %>).</p>
    <p><strong>Known distance classes:</strong> Known Pro, Known 40/45/50, W. Known Pro/40/45, Senior Known Pro/45/50, Senior Women's Known, Senior Master Known, Super Senior Known, Traditional, Barebow Recurve, Olympic Recurve, Crossbow, all Young Adult classes, all Youth classes, all Eagle classes, Junior Eagle, Youth Barebow</p>
    <p><strong>Unknown distance classes:</strong> Open Pro, Semi Pro, all Men's Open/Hunter/Pins classes, W. Open 45, W. Pins 30/40, Senior Hunter 45, Senior Open, Senior Master, Senior Pins, Senior Legends, Super Senior, Super Senior Pins</p>

    <h2>📏 ASA Max Yardage Bonus</h2>
    <p>Classes with longer max yardage are harder — you get a bonus for shooting farther:</p>
    <table class="weight-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Max Yardage</th><th>Bonus</th><th>Example Classes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>50 yards</td><td><strong>1.07×</strong></td><td>Open Pro, Semi Pro, Known 50, Known Pro, Senior Known 50</td></tr>
        <tr><td>45 yards</td><td><strong>1.05×</strong></td><td>Men's Open 45, Hunter 45, Known 45, W. Known 45, Senior Known 45</td></tr>
        <tr><td>40 yards</td><td><strong>1.03×</strong></td><td>Men's Open 40, Pins 40, Known 40, W. Known 40, Barebow Recurve, Olympic Recurve, Crossbow</td></tr>
        <tr><td>30 yards &amp; below</td><td>1.0× (baseline)</td><td>Pins 30, Senior Pins, Traditional, Eagle, Junior Eagle</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 id="national-records">🇺🇸 National Records: the Bar for TFAA Rounds</h2>
    <p>Every TFAA round is scored against the NFAA national record for its style: the best adult record, men's or women's, from NFAA's national records (<a href="https://nfaausa.com/files/indoor-national-records-120825152848.pdf" style="color:var(--accent-gold);">indoor</a>, updated August 2025, and <a href="https://nfaausa.com/files/field-national-records-080725200516.pdf" style="color:var(--accent-gold);">field and hunter</a>, updated July 2025). The record already shows how hard each style is, so these rounds get no equipment bonus.</p>
    <table class="weight-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Style</th><th>Indoor (two days, of 600)</th><th>Field (of 560)</th><th>Hunter (of 560)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
<% Object.keys(records).forEach(function (code) { %>        <tr><td><strong><%= code %></strong> (<%= rules.nationalRecordStyles[code] %>)</td><td><%= records[code].indoor %></td><td><%= records[code].field %></td><td><%= records[code].hunter %></td></tr>
<% }) %>      </tbody>
    </table>
    <ul>
      <li>An ordinary indoor round is out of 300, so its bar is half the two-day record. A State Indoor total meets the whole record.</li>
      <li>A SYWAT round can be a field or a hunter round, so its bar is the average of the two records. A State Outdoor total counts as one field and one hunter round, each against its own record.</li>
      <li>BBC is NFAA's Barebow style. NFAA keeps no record for two TFAA styles, so each meets the record of the nearest NFAA style: <strong>MBH</strong> (Modern Bowhunter Freestyle) meets the <%= rules.recordStyleFor.MBH %> record, and <strong>Genesis</strong> meets the <%= rules.recordStyleFor.GEN %> record. Like every style with a record, they get no equipment bonus.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="example">
      <strong>Example:</strong> a barebow recurve archer shoots 470 on a SYWAT round. The bar is (<%= records.BBR.field %> + <%= records.BBR.hunter %>) ÷ 2 = <%= barebowBar %>, so NPS = 470 ÷ <%= barebowBar %> × 1000 × <%= outdoorWeight.toFixed(2) %> = <strong><%= barebowNps %></strong>. A freestyle archer needs about 540 of <%= records.FS.field %> to match it: 540 ÷ <%= records.FS.field %> × 1000 × <%= outdoorWeight.toFixed(2) %> = <strong><%= freestyleNps %></strong>.
    </div>

    <h2 id="asa-bars">🎯 Texas ASA Class Bars</h2>
    <p>Each Texas ASA class has its own bar: the best score shot in that class in the three seasons TAGR reads. Tying it is worth 1000 before weights and bonuses. No bar is set below <%= rules.asaPar %>, which is par (a 10 on every target), so a class that only a few archers shoot is not measured against a low score.</p>
<% if (asaClasses.length > 0) { %>    <table class="weight-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Bar (of 360)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
<% asaClasses.forEach(function (name) { %>        <tr><td><%= name %></td><td><%= asaBars[name] %></td></tr>
<% }) %>      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The bars follow the results: a better score in a class raises that class's bar at the next rebuild.</p>
<% } %>
    <h2 id="equipment-bonuses">🏅 Equipment Bonuses</h2>
    <p>Texas ASA rounds are not scored against a record for their own equipment, so harder equipment gets a bonus:</p>
    <table class="weight-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Bonus</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Barebow Recurve</strong>, Youth Barebow (ASA)</td><td><strong>+8%</strong></td><td>Fingers + no sight</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Traditional</strong> (ASA)</td><td><strong>+11%</strong></td><td>Fingers + no sight, and no arrow rest (off the shelf) for an additional +3%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Olympic Recurve</strong> (ASA)</td><td><strong>+3%</strong></td><td>Finger release + sight</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Every other ASA class</td><td>Baseline</td><td>Compound with sight and release</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>🏅 Other Bonuses</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>X-Count Bonus (Indoor only)</strong> — Up to 5% extra for hitting more X rings. A 300-60X ranks higher than 300-0X. Formula: <code>NPS × (1 + xCount/60 × 0.05)</code></li>
      <li><strong>State Championship</strong> — <%= stateBonus %>% bonus for a State Championship round, reflecting the higher level of competition. It is the same in Texas ASA, TFAA Outdoor and TFAA Indoor.</li>
      <li><strong>How State Championships count</strong> — The TFAA Indoor State total is one round scored out of 600. The TFAA Outdoor State total is the field and hunter rounds together, so it counts as two rounds. A State result counts once per season in each class.</li>
      <li><strong>Scores above the maximum</strong> — A TFAA Outdoor round is scored out of 560, and the two State rounds together out of 1120. A few listed scores are higher than that. They are left out of the rankings for now, until it is clear how they were scored.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="field">⚔️ Who You Beat at State</h2>
    <p>A <strong>State Championship</strong> round counts for more when it beat ranked archers in the same class there. Beating a highly ranked archer adds much more than beating a low ranked one. Ordinary shoots (SYWAT rounds and Texas ASA qualifiers) earn nothing for this.</p>
    <table class="weight-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Archer you beat</th><th>Adds to the round</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>The best-ranked archer in TAGR</td><td><strong>+<%= (rules.fieldBeatTop * 100).toFixed(1) %>%</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>An archer three quarters of the way up the ranking</td><td>+<%= (rules.fieldBeatTop * Math.pow(0.75, rules.fieldStandingPower) * 100).toFixed(1) %>%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>An archer in the middle of the ranking</td><td>+<%= (rules.fieldBeatTop * Math.pow(0.5, rules.fieldStandingPower) * 100).toFixed(1) %>%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>An archer near the bottom, or without a full ranking yet</td><td>about nothing</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <ul>
      <li>The amounts for everybody you beat are added up, and a round's bonus stops at <strong><%= Math.round(rules.fieldBonusMax * 100) %>%</strong>.</li>
      <li>An archer's standing is where they are in the whole TAGR ranking before this bonus: 1 for the best, 0.5 for the middle. Each archer beaten adds <%= (rules.fieldBeatTop * 100).toFixed(1) %>% × standing<sup><%= rules.fieldStandingPower %></sup>.</li>
      <li>An equal score beats nobody, and a class you shot alone earns nothing. Losing to somebody costs nothing.</li>
      <li>The field is everybody with a State score in your class (Texas ASA) or your division (TFAA) that year.</li>
      <li>Open an archer on the rankings page to see how many archers each State round beat and what it added.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="recency">🕒 Newer Rounds Count for More</h2>
    <p>TAGR reads three seasons of each organization. A round counts in full in the newest season, and for a little less each season before:</p>
    <table class="weight-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Season</th><th>Weight</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Newest season of the organization</td><td><strong><%= times(recency[0]) %></strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>The season before</td><td><strong><%= times(recency[1]) %></strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Two seasons back</td><td><strong><%= times(recency[2]) %></strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Each organization has its own newest season, so a Texas ASA archer is not marked down because the TFAA indoor season started later in the year.</p>

    <h2>📈 Your TAGR Score</h2>
    <div class="formula-box">TAGR = (Sum of Top 5 NPS ÷ 5) × Event Count Bonus × Org Diversity Bonus × Title Bonus</div>
    <p>Your TAGR score is <strong>always divided by 5</strong> — even if you have fewer than 5 events. Empty slots count as zero. This prevents someone with one lucky tournament from outranking consistent archers.</p>

    <h2>📅 Minimum Events</h2>
    <p>You need at least <strong>3 events</strong> to get a full ranking. Archers with fewer than 3 events are shown as <strong style="color:#e74c3c;">PROVISIONAL</strong> — visible in the rankings but without a rank number.</p>

    <h2>🔄 Event Count Bonus</h2>
    <p>Shooting more events earns a small bonus that rewards consistency and commitment. It goes by the events of your <strong>busiest year</strong>, not the three years added together. A year is the indoor winter and the outdoor and Texas ASA seasons that follow it.</p>
    <table class="weight-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Events in your busiest year</th><th>Bonus</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>3 events or fewer</td><td>1.0× (baseline)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4 events</td><td>1.02×</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5 events</td><td>1.04×</td></tr>
        <tr><td>6–7 events</td><td>1.05×</td></tr>
        <tr><td>8+ events</td><td>1.06× (cap)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>🌐 Organization Diversity Bonus</h2>
    <p>Archers who prove themselves across <strong>multiple organizations</strong> in their top 5 scoring rounds earn a bonus. Scoring well in different formats (3D, field, indoor) is harder than repeating one.</p>
    <table class="weight-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Unique Orgs in Top 5</th><th>Bonus</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1 organization</td><td>1.0× (baseline)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2 organizations</td><td>1.03×</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3 organizations</td><td>1.05×</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>This counts the unique organizations among your actual top 5 NPS rounds — not just orgs you've shot with overall.</p>

    <h2 id="tournament-types">📍 Ranking by Tournament Type</h2>
    <p>Under each name is the archer's rank in each kind of tournament on its own: <%= rules.tournamentTypes.map(function (t) { return '<strong>' + t.label + '</strong> (' + t.org + ')'; }).join(', ') %>. It is worked out from that kind of round only, against the archers of the same <strong>class</strong>: the same age group (adult or youth) and the same equipment class (Compound, Barebow, Traditional, Olympic or Longbow), as on the class tabs. So #1 Field on a Barebow archer means the best adult or youth Barebow archer in field.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The score for each type is the average of an archer's best <strong><%= rules.typeRankBest %></strong> rounds of it, using the same NPS as the TAGR score.</li>
      <li>An archer needs at least <strong><%= rules.typeRankMinRounds %></strong> rounds of a type for a rank in it. With fewer, the line shows how many rounds they have; with none, a dash.</li>
      <li>These ranks are for information. They do not change the TAGR score or the main ranking.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>👑 TFAA State Record Holders</h2>
    <p>Archers who hold a current TFAA state record (Indoor, Outdoor, or 900 Round) are identified with a 👑 crown badge next to their name. Hover the crown to see which record(s) they hold.</p>
    <p>Records are scraped from <a href="https://texasfieldarchery.org/records/indoor" style="color:var(--accent-gold);">texasfieldarchery.org</a> and matched by name. Only the <strong>current</strong> record holder matters — if scores are tied, the most recent year wins.</p>

    <h2 id="titles">🏆 Shooters of the Year and ⭐ State Champions</h2>
    <p>A <strong>🏆 trophy</strong> next to a name marks a Shooter of the Year, and a <strong>⭐ star</strong> a State Champion. TFAA titles go back to the 2015-2016 season, the oldest TFAA still publishes standings for, and Texas ASA titles to 2024. A number beside the icon counts the titles. Hover the icon, or open the archer, to see which ones.</p>
    <p>Titles add to the TAGR score:</p>
    <table class="weight-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Title</th><th>Bonus</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>🏆 Shooter of the Year</td><td><strong><%= times(rules.soyTitleBonus) %></strong> (<%= pct(rules.soyTitleBonus) %>)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>⭐ State Champion</td><td><strong><%= times(rules.stateTitleBonus) %></strong> (<%= pct(rules.stateTitleBonus) %>)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Both</td><td><strong><%= (rules.soyTitleBonus * rules.stateTitleBonus).toFixed(4) %>×</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <ul>
      <li>Only a title from the three seasons TAGR ranks earns the bonus. Earlier titles are shown and counted beside your name, without a bonus.</li>
      <li>Each bonus counts once, however many titles of that kind you hold.</li>
      <li>A title earns the bonus only when it was won against somebody: at least <%= rules.titleBonusMinField %> archers were in the running for it. A title in a class of one still shows its icon.</li>
      <li>The bonus goes to your ranking in the equipment class the title was won in.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>How the titles are decided:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><span class="badge asa">TX ASA</span> <strong>Shooter of the Year</strong> — the best TOTAL SCORE in a class on the Texas ASA Shooter of the Year sheet (the best five qualifiers plus the State Championship score). Texas ASA gives the award only in a class where at least <%= rules.asaSoyMinArchers %> archers have five scores. <strong>State Champion</strong> — the archer placed first in the class in Texas ASA's results of the State Championship (2026 on). For a year TAGR does not have those results for, it is the best score in the sheet's State column, which Texas ASA fills for some classes only. Both titles appear once the State Championship has been shot.</li>
      <li><span class="badge tfaa">TFAA Outdoor</span> <span class="badge indoor">TFAA Indoor</span> <strong>Shooter of the Year</strong> — the SYWAT Aggregate award: the best total of three SYWAT scores plus the State Championship score in a division (age group and style). TFAA requires all four scores. <strong>State Champion</strong> — the best State Championship score in the division. Indoors, X counts break ties.</li>
      <li>For seasons before the ranked ones, the State Champion is the archer placed first in the division in TFAA's results of the State Championship itself.</li>
      <li>A title shows on the archer's ranking for the equipment class it was won in, and two archers with exactly the same score both hold it. An earlier title won in an equipment class you no longer shoot shows on the rankings you do have.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>🤝 Ties</h2>
    <p>Archers with the same TAGR score share a rank, and the next rank skips past them: two archers tied at 6 are followed by rank 8.</p>

    <h2>💡 Example</h2>
    <div class="example">
      <strong>Archer A</strong> shoots 6 ASA unknown distance events at 45 yards this year, scoring NPS: 850, 840, 830, 820, 810, 800<br>
      Top 5 average = (850 + 840 + 830 + 820 + 810) ÷ 5 = 830<br>
      Event bonus (6 events) = 1.05×<br>
      1 org = 1.0× diversity bonus<br>
      <strong>TAGR = 830 × 1.05 × 1.0 = 872</strong><br><br>
      
      <strong>Archer B</strong> shoots 8 events in one year across TX ASA + TFAA Outdoor + TFAA Indoor<br>
      Top 5 NPS: 920, 910, 890, 880, 870 (from all 3 orgs)<br>
      Top 5 average = 894<br>
      Event bonus (8 events) = 1.06×<br>
      3 orgs in top 5 = 1.05× diversity bonus<br>
      <strong>TAGR = 894 × 1.06 × 1.05 = 995</strong><br><br>

      <strong>Archer C</strong> shoots 1 indoor event, scoring NPS: 900<br>
      Top 5 sum = 900 + 0 + 0 + 0 + 0 = 900 ÷ 5 = 180<br>
      <strong>TAGR = 180 (PROVISIONAL — needs 2 more events)</strong>
    </div>

    <h2>🏹 Equipment Classes</h2>
    <p>Archers are ranked within their equipment class. If you shoot multiple classes, you get a separate ranking for each.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong style="color:#e74c3c;">Compound</strong> — FS, FSL, BHFS, MBH, BBC, Genesis + ASA Known/Open/Hunter/Pins/Pro classes</li>
      <li><strong style="color:#2ecc71;">Barebow</strong> — BBR, Barebow Recurve, Youth Barebow</li>
      <li><strong style="color:#e67e22;">Traditional</strong> — TRAD, Traditional</li>
      <li><strong style="color:#3498db;">Olympic</strong> — FSLR, Olympic Recurve</li>
      <li><strong style="color:#f1c40f;">Longbow</strong> — LB</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>📡 Data Sources (3-Year Window)</h2>
    <p>TAGR pulls the last 3 years of competition data to build comprehensive rankings:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><span class="badge asa">TX ASA</span> — <strong>2024, 2025, 2026</strong> individual tournament results from Texas ASA Google Sheets (SOY + Tournament Results Archive)</li>
      <li><span class="badge tfaa">TFAA Outdoor</span> — the <strong>3 most recent seasons</strong> with scores, read from texasfieldarchery.org</li>
      <li><span class="badge indoor">TFAA Indoor</span> — the <strong>3 most recent seasons</strong> with scores, read from texasfieldarchery.org</li>
      <li><span class="badge asa">TX ASA</span> — the results of the <strong>State Championship</strong> (2026 on): every archer who shot State gets the State round, whether or not they are on the Shooter of the Year sheet</li>
      <li><em>TSAA — Coming soon</em></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Rankings are rebuilt every Tuesday morning (Central time). Cross-organization names are automatically matched and merged (e.g., "SMITH, JOHN" from TFAA + "JOHN SMITH" from ASA = same archer). Extra spaces, full stops and where "Jr." is written make no difference, and two spellings on the same ASA number (Ken and Kenneth) are one archer when they share a first or last name. Two different people with exactly the same name still cannot be told apart.</p>
    <p><strong>Adult or Youth</strong> goes by an archer's newest results: once they shoot a TFAA season in an adult age group, they move to the Adult list with all of their scores.</p>

